[
{"claim": "headsup renders remote-sourced text - result.msg lines, .upgradelist library chips, address chips - with textContent, never innerHTML", "detail": "F3 (commit a3aa0206), same rule as peer.peer: anything a peer or the network supplies is untrusted. me.update's address chips and the upgrade-list chips are DOM-built with textContent; error msgs are appended as text nodes. The rule applies to every future line touched in this control, not just the F3 spots.", "tags": "peer.headsup,xss,security,F3", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "headsup", "facet": "js", "hash": "b7346f6136d1595e"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793417931028},
{"claim": "#headsupdisplay is one reused container across orb focuses, so state classes set on it (isadmin) leak into the next peer's panel unless reset at mount.", "detail": "Found on the rig during F7 QA (commit 3a3ae14a, peers-app-fixes): an admin peer's panel earned .isadmin, then a refusing peer's panel opened in the same #headsupdisplay and still showed every admin-only element. The mount handler now strips state classes before wiring. Any future headsup state carried as a class or dataset on the panel root needs the same reset-at-mount discipline.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,ui,mount,state-leak,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793484067319},
{"claim": "To exercise the HUD panel without driving the 3D scene, mount peer.headsup directly into #headsupdisplay - that is all onSceneFocus does when an orb gains focus.", "detail": "Used in the F10 panel QA because the scene-host api handle is awkward to reach from a test script; the direct mount runs the identical code path the orb-focus flow runs, so panel behavior (chips, updater, summaries) is fully testable headlessly this way.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,testing,qa,unreviewed", "confidence": "medium", "time": 1793485879002},
{"claim": "Stubbing peer data for HUD tests must drive #headsupdisplay's api directly - wrapping the json() global never intercepts the panel's 10s poll", "detail": "The panel's periodic peer re-read calls the closure-scoped send_peers binding, not the json global, so a json() stub is bypassed. Working recipe: mutate the orb's live DATA record (e.g. set address:'' and remote_address) and call document.getElementById('headsupdisplay').api.update() - that api object IS the panel's me. Restore the record afterwards. Found while testing F13's remote_address fallback.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,qa,testing,stub,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793518275981},
{"claim": "The .hideme utility class cannot hide the HUD's round buttons on its own: the shared app css declares .roundbutton-small { display: inline-flex } after .hideme at equal specificity, so .hideme's display:none loses the cascade — hiding such a button needs a more specific rule (headsup now carries .hudpanel .closeaddressbutton.hideme { display:none }).", "detail": "Root cause of the F13 stray ✕ (fixed in commit cd43ddeb, journal patch p43): the close-address button was never actually hidden by .hideme; it merely sat invisible under the ⋯ button while .abspos stacked them, and became visible the moment Phase 4 removed .abspos. The expand/collapse click handlers set inline style.display, which outranks the new rule, so toggling still works. General lesson: any element combining .hideme with a class the app css later gives an explicit display value will render; check computed style, not class lists.", "tags": "peer.headsup,css,hideme,roundbutton,cascade,specificity,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793521886147},
{"claim": "Scripted instant close-then-reopen of the peer HUD races the close animation: the close's async onfinish fires after the reopen and stamps display:none onto the hud, blanking the fresh panel — a test-harness artifact, not a product bug.", "detail": "Hit during Phase 5b step-4 verification (close panel, immediately reopen via hostData.onFocus). Real users cannot trigger it: the 300ms close animation finishes before an orb can be re-tapped, and watchHud guards. Test scripts driving the HUD must either wait out the close animation before reopening or restore display after the stale onfinish lands.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,testing,animation,race,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793521892425},
{"claim": "Pixel sampling proves only that the expected content is present — it cannot reveal the unexpected. When screenshots cannot be viewed, every control in the photographed region must also be checked for visibility (getComputedStyle display, getBoundingClientRect), not just the elements the test case names.", "detail": "Phase 5 of peers-app-fixes marked M22 PASS while its own collapsed shot showed a stray orange ✕ (a Phase 4 regression): the sampler counted the pixels the case expected and had no way to notice an extra button. The 5b fix (cd43ddeb) was only found by a human viewing the image. Standing QA rule for this rig: enumerate the interactive elements in the shot region and assert each one's computed visibility matches the state being photographed.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,qa,screenshots,pixel-sampling,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793522133686},
{"claim": "Evidence screenshots must be produced by real user actions with the window visible (document.hidden false); states manufactured by directly toggling classes or styles photograph UI no user can reach. For tabbed UI, assert the tab header and the content agree (.navbar-tab1.selected together with .tab1.selected) before shooting.", "detail": "The Phase 5b m22 retakes were taken while document.hidden with the Info CONTENT forced visible by script after buildControls had auto-switched the panel header to Dashboard — a header/content combination no in-page click can produce (initNavbar toggles both sides synchronously). The byte-identical files were the tell. Phase 6 retook them with the window visible, Info clicked as a user, and both selected-class pairs verified in agreement. Related quirk: agent.browser.act refuses navbar-tab divs as 'disabled' (same as <tr> rows) — use the plain click tool.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,qa,screenshots,document.hidden,tabs,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793522141739},
{"claim": "The headsup panel's tabs are not isolated from the page's: app ui initNavbar clears 'selected' from every .tab-content under its el scope, so clicking the Add Connection dialog's LAN/Connect tabs (page scope) blanks the panel's content while its header stays highlighted; and buildControls in peer.headsup clicks document.querySelector('.navbar-tab2') document-wide, auto-yanking every fresh panel to Dashboard.", "detail": "Measured on the rig (MEDIUM Phase 6, 2026-11-01): with the panel on Info, Add Connection → Connect left navbar-tab1 selected but .tab1 content stripped (display none) — a real-user header/content mismatch; master has the same code. The full fix (scoping initNavbar's content sweep to the tabs' own container) is a shared app change for Marc; the buildControls lookup could be scoped to ME in peer.headsup. Filed in 3-LOW.md 'To check'. Any control that mounts tabbed sub-controls inside a tabbed page inherits this hazard.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,app.ui,initNavbar,tabs,scope,unreviewed", "confidence": "low", "time": 1793522149115, "adjudicated": 1793885743405},
{"claim": "Stubbing app/read replies in page tests must match the POST body, not the URL: the record-id discriminator (e.g. id=controls_shared) rides in the POST data, so a URL-only matcher silently passes every request through unstubbed.", "detail": "Found during peers LOW Phase 1 while stubbing controls_shared/controls_available failures for F16: the correct stub predicate is a match against url + ' ' + postData. This corrects the §3.4 stub recipe in the peers-app-fixes spec, which matched the URL alone and therefore appeared to stub while actually intercepting nothing.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,qa,stubbing,app-read,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793525474520},
{"claim": "peer.headsup remembers the user's tab pick per peer on the host element: buildControls clicks only ME's own .navbar-tab2, a refresh restores the remembered tab, and only a never-opened peer starts on Dashboard (Phase 1b).", "detail": "Commit fa4070e4 (peers-app-fixes). The old code clicked document.querySelector('.navbar-tab2') document-wide, yanking every open/refresh to Dashboard and risking a click on some other control's tab bar. The pick is stored on the reused #headsupdisplay host keyed by peer uuid, so it survives the panel's rebuild-on-refresh but resets with the page. The page-scope half of the hazard is still open: the shared app ui initNavbar sweeps 'selected' off every .tab-content under the page root (the Add Connection tabs blank the panel's content) — shared data/app code, deferred to Marc.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,tabs,buildControls,1b", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "headsup", "facet": "js", "hash": "e7118e6be3f9b4eb"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793532074730},
{"claim": "The panel's update chips must size to their content: headsup's own .ispos rule made each chip an inline-block stretched to calc(100% - 20px), which clipped the ✓ over the version and stretched the ⚠ chip; the dialog's content-sized chips are the model (Phase 1b).", "detail": "Commit 90a6539f (peers-app-fixes). Symptom seen in session 2's shots: 'app v123 ➤ 12✓' with the ✓ covering the digits, and '⚠ different author' chips with a wide internal gap. Keep panel chips free of fixed or minimum widths; if a chip misrenders, check headsup's own css overrides before suspecting the shared chip markup that peer.peer's updater builds.", "tags": "peer.headsup,chips,css,1b", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "headsup", "facet": "css", "hash": "6f9278b25a6f9d78"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793532078841},
{"claim": "The panel's layout contract (Phase 7): one .hudheader flex row (✕ · name/UUID · dots+latency · ⋮) and a .hudinfogrid two-column grid share one inner gutter (--hud-gutter, 24px = the tab bodies' .padme), and the admin-only Platform block joins the grid via display:contents.", "detail": "Commits 3dd81fbf + 982443e8 (peers-app-fixes). ✕ and the name both sit on the 32px name line (shared vertical centre); the UUID sits under the name at the name's left edge; the ⋮ menu card anchors in .hudheadright (position:relative). Grid: labels (and the key/lock icons, glyphs pulled back 6px) in column 1 at x24, values in column 2 at x136; .hudinfofull spans both columns (address list, crate form/status, Stay connected). .hudpanel.isadmin .hudcrates{display:contents} must stay AFTER the .isadmin .adminonly revert rule (equal specificity, source order decides), and initCrates' remove() of .hudcrates still drops all its grid rows at once. Empty Info values render '—' (js), the key row's failed check says 'unknown', and the ⋯ carries title 'Show all addresses (N)' with a dim +N beside the IP when the peer reported more than one. Don't re-wrap the tab contents in a container with its own padding - that was the old double-indent.", "tags": "peer.headsup,layout,css,grid,header,phase7", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "headsup", "facet": "css", "hash": "fc4dbcb547e078e0"}, "confidence": "medium", "time": 1793540426465, "adjudicated": 1793543723553},
{"claim": "The panel's layout contract (Phase 7c): the .hudheader flex row and the .hudinfogrid two-column grid share one inner gutter (--hud-gutter, 24px), and the admin-only Platform rows are DIRECT grid children, each tagged .hudcrates.adminonly — never a display:contents wrapper.", "detail": "Phase 7c (peers-app-fixes) replaced Phase 7's .hudpanel.isadmin .hudcrates{display:contents} wrapper: Gecko (Noobscape/Firefox 128) kept stale grid track heights for the wrapper's children when #headsupdisplay was dragged narrower with Info visible, painting the crate form over the Platform value's second line and the key row (after-7b-panel-min-360-info) while getBoundingClientRect stayed healthy. Flattened: the Platform label, .hud_crates value, .hudcrateform and .hudcratestatus each carry .hudcrates (initCrates' failure path removes them all via querySelectorAll) and .adminonly. .hudpanel.isadmin .hudcrateform{display:flex} (flex-wrap:wrap, gap 8px) must stay AFTER the .isadmin .adminonly revert rule (equal specificity, source order decides), and .hudpanel .hudcratestatus.hideme{display:none} keeps the status div hidden for admins until a run shows it inline (the revert otherwise out-ranks .hideme — the closeaddressbutton precedent). Everything else from Phase 7 stands: labels and the key/lock glyphs in column 1 at x24, values in column 2 at x136, .hudinfofull spans both columns, empty values render '—', the failed key check says 'unknown', and the ⋯ carries 'Show all addresses (N)' with a dim +N.", "tags": "peer.headsup,layout,css,grid,display-contents,phase7c", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "headsup", "facet": "css", "hash": "6d0e71fc0c87b6d2"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793543736432},
{"claim": "A pairwise getBoundingClientRect intersection check can pass while the painted pixels overlap: Gecko layout/paint staleness (display:contents grid children during a live panel resize) is only caught by analyzing the screenshot's pixels against the DOM rects.", "detail": "Phase 7c (peers-app-fixes): the 7b min-360 Info shot showed the crate inputs at y279 covering the Platform value's second line and the buttons over the key row, yet rect checks on the same code found nothing, and the stale state could not be reproduced synthetically — synchronous width loops and frame-separated width steps both relaid out correctly, because any forced layout read heals the stale tracks; only a real pointer drag's cadence triggered it. Verification recipe: take the live screenshot, navigate the driven tab to it via file:// (a chrome-privileged goto may leave http for file:), draw it on a canvas, band-profile the panel region per row (text/accent/mid pixel counts), and compare band edges with the recorded getBoundingClientRect values — they must agree within antialiasing noise.", "tags": "peer.headsup,qa,testing,screenshot,paint,rects,phase7c", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793543741953},
{"claim": "The headsup panel's update chips resolve through the shared document.body.peerPackage reader with a deliberate fail-open fallback: when peer.peer's js has not published the reader, every lib stays 'unknown, allow'.", "detail": "The panel does not own a package reader; it consumes the one peer.peer installs. Mounted without it (or before it exists), chips behave as pre-U2 - nothing is dimmed or excluded, and zero lib_info calls are made. So nopkg gating is only in force when the constellation page's js ran.", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,updates,u2,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793566315417},
{"claim": "The headsup panel's pinned header-and-tabs block must sit just below the modal backdrop and popups in the stacking order; if it sits above them, the header stays clickable through an open modal like Add Control.", "detail": "D1, Phase 1 of peers-dashboard (commit 37f03bd9): the first version raised the sticky block above everything except popups, so the Add Control backdrop did not cover the header. The old floating tab bar had the same flaw. Tab bars that dashboard tiles bring with them (camera LIVE/DVR/EVENTS) scroll with their tile inside the panel.", "tags": "peer.headsup,z-index,sticky,modal,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793878531833},
{"claim": "Dashboard tiles size their content from the slot's dimensions, so a slot with auto height collapses them: the camera tile's adjustSizes() fits its image to the slot's height, and the Reboot tile draws its canvas from the slot's size.", "detail": "Found in D4, 2026-11. A big tile needs a minimum height (16:9 of its width) or the camera image collapses to nothing. Icon tiles stay a fixed 150x150. Big tiles carry overflow-y:auto, because the camera's DVR tab needs about 391px of its own controls; in a 176px slot (360px panel) those controls otherwise spill over the next tile.", "tags": "peer.headsup,camera.camera,dashboard,layout,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793880301002},
{"claim": "A CSS right offset on the panel's popup card (.control-settings-popup) has no effect: app.ui opens the card position:fixed with an inline left taken from the click point, so placement has to be corrected after initPopups by a click listener that sets left.", "detail": "In 48c867e2 the ⋮ card's right edge is lined up with the tab bar's right edge by zeroing .popupcard margin inside the panel and adding a ⋮ click listener after initPopups. This is the panel-side workaround for the unfixed app.ui clamp, which ignores the card's 20px margin.", "tags": "peer.headsup,app.ui,popup,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793880309295},
{"claim": "Unicode glyph characters on buttons (←, →, ✕, ✓) render as empty boxes or a plain 'x' in the peer app's UI font — button glyphs must use the kit's SVG icons instead (../app/asset/app/icons/ui/dark/arrow-left.svg, close.svg, check.svg), mirroring arrow-left with a CSS scaleX(-1) for →.", "detail": "Found in the Phase 3 review of the peers-dashboard work (after-D5-editmode shots showed all three edit-bar glyphs as empty boxes) and fixed in Phase 4 commit bebae7db: the edit bar's buttons became 32px round transparent buttons with a 20px SVG glyph matching the header ✕, titles/aria-labels unchanged. The same reasoning preempted a text ✓ in D6's 'Added' marker (check.svg used instead). Verified drawing at the 680px and 360px panel widths.", "tags": "peer.headsup,icons,frontend,fonts,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793883926580},
{"claim": "closeonclick is peer.headsup's own menu convention - app.ui's non-modal popup has no menu-item close concept, so the panel itself must bind EVERY .closeonclick row", "detail": "app.ui closes a non-modal card only on clicks outside it; the ⋮ menu rows' closeonclick is peer.headsup's own idea, and the js bound it with ME.querySelector('.closeonclick') (singular), so only the first row (Refresh) ever closed the card - picking Add Control left the menu open behind the modal and after it closed. Fixed on peers-dashboard commit 12d8cc56 by binding all rows.", "tags": "peer.headsup,app.ui,popup,menu,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793885735340},
{"claim": "container-type on a dashboard ancestor traps child controls' position:fixed modals - it makes the element a containing block and stacking context, so a tile's modal positions against the container and loses to page-level backdrops; the big tile's 16:9 minimum is a floated ::before strut instead", "detail": "D4 first used container-type: inline-size on .hudapps for the tile sizing. The camera tile's own ⚙ settings modal (position:fixed, opened via app.ui) then opened under app.ui's .greyedout backdrop, half off-screen and unclickable (P04). Fixed in peers-dashboard commit b9a167cc: drop container-type entirely and express the big tile's 16:9 minimum as a zero-width floated ::before strut (padding-top: calc(100% * 9/16)); the tile slot's overflow:auto BFC contains the float, so the tile's height still follows its content. Tile layout stayed pixel-identical after the swap. Same lesson filed in kb.frontend in general CSS terms.", "tags": "peer.headsup,css,container-queries,stacking-context,modal,position-fixed,D4,P04,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793895256869},
{"claim": "The panel's INFO/DASHBOARD tabs use hudtab-info/hudtab-dash ids and peer.headsup's own two-tab switcher, never ui.initNavbar, because initNavbar selects tab content by class across its whole subtree and reaches into mounted tiles' tabs", "detail": "ui.initNavbar(el) (data/app, don't edit) handles a click on a tab with data-id='tab2' by removing 'selected' from every .tab-content under el and adding it to every .tab2 under el. The panel used to call initNavbar(ME) with generic tab1/tab2 ids, and the camera tile mounted inside uses tab1/tab2/tab3 for LIVE/DVR/EVENTS - so clicking DASHBOARD also selected the camera's DVR content, and panel tab clicks stripped the tile's tab state (D9). Fixed in peers-dashboard commit 37c11c4c: the panel's tabs got ids no control would pick (hudtab-info/hudtab-dash), a small panel-local switcher toggles only the panel's own two tab contents and never touches .tab-content inside .hudapps, and an old stored tab1/tab2 pick (the 1b per-peer tab memory) is mapped to the new ids. Any control that mounts tabbed sub-controls must avoid generic tab ids and subtree-wide initNavbar scope.", "tags": "peer.headsup,app.ui,initNavbar,tabs,scope,D9,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793895269449},
{"claim": "app.ui popups open at the triggering click's clientX/clientY, so a synthetic .click() (coordinates 0,0) opens the ⋮ menu at the window's top-left corner - tests must dispatch a MouseEvent at the button's centre", "detail": "Seen in before-P15 (peers-dashboard QA): a bare element.click() carries clientX/clientY 0,0 and app.ui placed the card there. Every popup the Peers page and panel open (⋮ menu, Add Control, Add Connection, Update Peers) is positioned this way. QA recipe: compute the button's bounding-box centre and dispatch new MouseEvent('click', {clientX, clientY, bubbles: true}) there. The card also animates out from the click point, so measure only the settled card (finish its animations or poll for stability).", "tags": "peer.headsup,peer.peer,app.ui,popup,qa,testing,real-coordinate-clicks,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793895284060},
{"end": true}]
